Yes. Now that we have all three files, we can connect the whole picture. The key thing to understand is that these YAML files are not three independent things — they form one chain:

Deployment → Pods ← Service ← Ingress

But when kubectl apply processes the folder, the important part is how the resources eventually connect, not necessarily which YAML file Kubernetes "creates first."

Let's use your production namespace as the example.

1. Starting point

You have:

kubernetes/production/
├── deployment.yaml
├── service.yaml
└── ingress.yaml

And your workflow eventually runs:

kubectl apply -f kubernetes/production

Before this, your production namespace already exists:

production

And your NGINX Ingress Controller is already running in:

ingress-nginx
2. Deployment is created

Your deployment.yaml says:

kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: solar-system
  namespace: production

Kubernetes creates:

production
└── Deployment: solar-system

But the Deployment itself doesn't run your application.

Its job is to create and manage Pods.

Your Deployment says:

replicas: 3

for example.

So the Deployment creates:

Deployment: solar-system
        │
        ├── Pod 1
        ├── Pod 2
        └── Pod 3
3. Deployment creates Pods

Inside your Deployment you have:

template:
  metadata:
    labels:
      app: solar-system

Therefore every Pod created by this Deployment gets:

app=solar-system

So:

Deployment
    │
    ├── Pod 1
    │     └── app=solar-system
    │
    ├── Pod 2
    │     └── app=solar-system
    │
    └── Pod 3
          └── app=solar-system

And your container runs:

containerPort: 3000

So your Node.js application is listening on port 3000 inside each Pod.

4. Now the Service is created

Your service.yaml says:

kind: Service
metadata:
  name: solar-system
  namespace: production

Kubernetes creates:

production
├── Deployment: solar-system
├── Pods
└── Service: solar-system

But how does the Service know which Pods belong to it?

This is the important connection.

Your Service has:

selector:
  app: solar-system

And your Pods have:

labels:
  app: solar-system

Therefore:

Service selector
      ↓
app=solar-system
      ↓
finds matching Pods

So the Service automatically discovers:

Service: solar-system
       │
       ├── Pod 1
       ├── Pod 2
       └── Pod 3
5. Service forwards traffic to port 3000

Your Service has:

ports:
- port: 3000
  targetPort: 3000

This means:

Service port 3000
       ↓
Pod targetPort 3000

So when the Service receives traffic on port 3000, it forwards it to the application running on port 3000 inside one of the matching Pods.

6. Now the Ingress is created

Your ingress.yaml creates:

production
├── Deployment: solar-system
├── Service: solar-system
└── Ingress: solar-system

Your Ingress contains something like:

backend:
  service:
    name: solar-system
    port:
      number: 3000

This creates the next connection:

Ingress
   ↓
Service: solar-system

So now the whole application chain is connected.

7. What happens when a user opens the application?

Suppose your final URL is:

https://solar-system-production.192.168.49.2.nip.io

The request goes:

                    INTERNET
                       │
                       ▼
             NGINX Ingress Controller
                 ingress-nginx
                       │
                       ▼
              Ingress: solar-system
                 production
                       │
                       ▼
              Service: solar-system
                 port 3000
                       │
              ┌────────┼────────┐
              ▼        ▼        ▼
            Pod 1    Pod 2    Pod 3
              │        │        │
              └────────┼────────┘
                       ▼
                 Node.js :3000

That's the complete traffic path.

8. Where does the Ingress IP fit?

Remember your workflow obtains:

kubectl -n ingress-nginx get svc ingress-nginx-controller

Suppose the IP is:

192.168.49.2

Your workflow puts that into the Ingress manifest.

The hostname becomes:

solar-system-production.192.168.49.2.nip.io

Now when the browser requests that hostname:

solar-system-production.192.168.49.2.nip.io

the request reaches:

192.168.49.2
      ↓
NGINX Ingress Controller

NGINX looks at the hostname and finds the matching Ingress rule:

solar-system-production...
        ↓
production/solar-system Service
