10. One very important correction

Don't think:

"Deployment creates first, then Service, then Ingress."

That's not something you should depend on.

When you run:

kubectl apply -f kubernetes/production

Kubernetes processes the manifests and creates/updates the resources.

The relationships are established through names, labels, selectors, and references:

Deployment
    │
    │ creates Pods
    ▼
Pods
    │
    │ labels: app=solar-system
    ▲
    │ selector
Service
    │
    │ backend reference
    ▼
Ingress

So even though the resources are logically connected, Kubernetes doesn't require you to manually create them one-by-one.

11. What your workflow is actually doing

Your production workflow roughly performs:

1. Get Ingress Controller IP
          ↓
2. Replace variables in YAML
          ↓
3. Create MongoDB Secret
          ↓
4. kubectl apply -f kubernetes/production
          ↓
       ┌──┴───────────────┐
       ▼                  ▼
 Deployment             Service
       │                  │
       ▼                  │
     Pods ◄───────────────┘
       ▲
       │
     Ingress
       ▲
       │
NGINX Ingress Controller

And your MongoDB connection is a separate connection:

Pod
 │
 │ envFrom
 ▼
mongo-db-creds Secret
 │
 ▼
MongoDB Server

So your application has two important infrastructure relationships:

Incoming traffic:

Internet
   ↓
Ingress Controller
   ↓
Ingress
   ↓
Service
   ↓
Pod


Database:

Pod
   ↓
Environment variables
   ↓
Kubernetes Secret
   ↓
MongoDB

That's the architecture you are building.
